            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall your house is relatively airtight, with only 3.2 air changes per hour measured on the day of the test. There are some ways that you could further reduce this. In addition, other upgrades recommended in this report may affect the air leakage rate,



I recommend outfitting any electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 



I also recommend that weather stripping along all of your exterior doors be checked regularly and replaced as necessary. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 



I was unable to check for air leakage using the infrared camera during my site visit, but I was able to check using other methods. I did note most of the air leakage was coming around some of the sliding windows. You will also likely see air leakage from the attic hatch, which I was not able to check.



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $60 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.7 GJ. </airSeal>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones, and you mentioned replacing some of the windows,  I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of your windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing all of your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $780 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 22.6 GJ.   



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 85% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler(direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



This upgrade will also affect your domestic hot water costs. 



I also recommend installing an electronic damper on your combustion air duct and turning down the heat in your garage.



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion, propane fired boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $1,820 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 45.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



If you are planning on replacing your pellet stove, I recommend you install a ULC Certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air duct. I have modeleld the savings associated with using your current pellet stove for 25% of your heating load. 



Savings:

By using your current pellet stove for 25% of your heating load, you could potentially save $700 per year in energy savings, and qualify for up to 1/2 of pre-tax purchase cost of the stove to a maximum of $1,000, from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
